<%# locals: (viewer:, editable:, save:) %>
<%# EIGHT BRANCHES, EIGHT PARTIALS UNDER files/viewers/ (stage 4, S4-5), AND A NINTH THE MAKER'S PAGES
    ALONE RENDER: the workbook grid (_spreadsheet, 2026-10-21), whose data comes from a session-authed
    door, so a visitor's page shows an .xlsx as the download fallback instead. Each takes every URL as
    a STRICT local, so the same eight render on a visitor's public file page pointed at public
    routes — a viewer that built its own URLs would be one condition with two answers across two
    pages, the shape §14a forbids. The chain itself is the MAKER's, because the branch ORDER is the
    maker's pages', and everything that is a fact about the OWNER rather than about the file stays on
    files/show: the repo banner, the recipe panels, History and Notes.

    ONE CHAIN FOR TWO MAKER PAGES (2026-10-21): the file page renders this, and so does the model
    page's pane, both from FileViewer#viewer_state_for, so the two cannot disagree about which viewer
    a file gets or what it is handed. IT LIVES OUTSIDE files/viewers/ ON PURPOSE: it calls private
    route helpers (blob_file_path, geometry_file_path, …), which architecture_invariants_test.rb's
    Public scan forbids inside that directory. A visitor's page keeps its own chain
    (public/files/view.html.erb) over the same eight partials. `editable:` and `save:` are the two
    facts about the OWNER's page the model pane switches off: the file page passes its own editable
    answer and true; the pane passes false for both. %>
<% if viewer.info.content_token.nil? %>
  <p class="muted">No content yet.</p>
<% elsif viewer.kind == :image %>
  <%= render "files/viewers/image", name: viewer.node.name, blob_url: blob_file_path(viewer.node.node_id) %>
<% elsif viewer.kind == :pdf %>
  <%= render "files/viewers/pdf", name: viewer.node.name, blob_url: blob_file_path(viewer.node.node_id) %>
<% elsif viewer.kind == :kicanvas %>
  <%= render "files/viewers/kicanvas", controls: "full",
             named_url: named_blob_file_path(viewer.node.node_id, filename: viewer.node.name) %>
<% elsif viewer.kind == :gerber %>
  <%= render "files/viewers/gerber", layers: viewer.gerber_layers, single: viewer.gerber_single,
             thumbnail: viewer.thumbnail, thumbnail_url: preview_src(viewer.thumbnail), name: viewer.node.name,
             download_url: blob_file_path(viewer.node.node_id, download: 1) %>
<% elsif viewer.kind == :text %>
  <%= render "files/viewers/code", blob_url: blob_file_path(viewer.node.node_id),
             ext: File.extname(viewer.node.name).downcase.delete("."), editable: editable,
             save_url: file_path(viewer.node.node_id), base_token: viewer.info.content_token %>
<% elsif viewer.kind == :spreadsheet %>
  <%# An .xlsx (2026-10-21): the grid fetches the workbook and saves edits through its own door,
      Files::SpreadsheetsController. `save:` decides whether the grid can save at all — the file page
      can, the model page's pane shows the workbook read-only. %>
  <%= render "files/viewers/spreadsheet", data_url: file_spreadsheet_path(viewer.node.node_id),
             save_url: (file_spreadsheet_path(viewer.node.node_id) if save),
             download_url: blob_file_path(viewer.node.node_id, download: 1),
             scripts: [ asset_path("jsuites.js"), asset_path("jspreadsheet.js") ],
             styles: [ asset_path("jsuites.css"), asset_path("jspreadsheet.css") ] %>
<% elsif viewer.kind == :model3d %>
  <%= render "files/viewers/model3d", name: viewer.node.name,
             named_url: named_blob_file_path(viewer.node.node_id, filename: viewer.node.name),
             format: File.extname(viewer.node.name).downcase.delete("."),
             colors: (viewer.manifest&.colors || []),
             unit_factor: (viewer.manifest&.unit_factor || 1.0),
             thumbnail: viewer.thumbnail, thumbnail_url: preview_src(viewer.thumbnail),
             download_url: blob_file_path(viewer.node.node_id, download: 1),
             preview_url: file_preview_path(viewer.node.node_id), viewer_preview: viewer.thumbnail&.viewer? %>
<% elsif viewer.kind == :measure %>
  <%# .lbrn/.lbrn2, .mage, .dxf, .svg — a picture at rest, with a button that swaps in a
      measurable outline view built from the geometry payload. The four save URLs are the
      maker's own doors (stage 4, S4-3): an export lands beside the file it came from, through
      whichever of the two upload flows this deployment uses. `save:` is what renders those
      two controls at all — the file page passes true; a VISITOR's file page passes false and gets
      the same bar without them (stage 4, S4-5), because there is no library for an export to land
      in, and the model page's pane passes false too, because saving is the file page's.

      `source_node_id`/`link_url` ARE WHERE A SAVED EXPORT CAME FROM (stage 4, S4-8): this file,
      and the door that records the fact. `__ID__` is a template measure_controller.js substitutes
      once the upload has answered with the new node's id — the partial takes the URL as a strict
      local rather than building one, like every other URL it needs (a shared viewer must not
      reach a private route helper; architecture_invariants_test.rb scans that directory). %>
  <%= render "files/viewers/measure", name: viewer.node.name,
             geometry_url: geometry_file_path(viewer.node.node_id),
             blob_url: blob_file_path(viewer.node.node_id), thumbnail: viewer.thumbnail,
             thumbnail_url: preview_src(viewer.thumbnail),
             download_url: blob_file_path(viewer.node.node_id, download: 1),
             save: save,
             port_writable: capabilities.port_writable?, create_url: files_path,
             begin_url: begin_upload_files_path, finalize_url: finalize_upload_files_path,
             parent_node_id: viewer.node.parent_node_id,
             source_node_id: viewer.node.node_id, link_url: file_links_path("__ID__") %>
<% else %>
  <%# THE TAIL: the ready thumbnail, the "still generating" while its job runs, and the download
      fallback. THREE elsifs COLLAPSED INTO ONE else, which is behaviour-preserving rather than a
      simplification — the partial re-tests `kind == :thumbnail` internally and the chain's final
      arm was already the catch-all. %>
  <%= render "files/viewers/static", kind: viewer.kind, thumbnail: viewer.thumbnail, name: viewer.node.name,
             thumbnail_url: preview_src(viewer.thumbnail),
             download_url: blob_file_path(viewer.node.node_id, download: 1) %>
<% end %>
